  "classIri": "urn:ngm:class:localisation",
  "domain": "robotics",
  "definition": "Localisation in robotics and autonomous systems is the process by which a mobile agent estimates its position (x, y, z) and orientation (roll, pitch, yaw) within a reference frame\u2014either a pre-built map or a simultaneously constructed one\u2014using data from onboard sensors such as LiDAR, cameras, IMUs, and wheel encoders processed through probabilistic inference algorithms. Accurate localisation is a prerequisite for autonomous navigation, path planning, and manipulation tasks, as control decisions depend critically on knowing where the robot is relative to its goal and obstacles. The joint problem of localisation and map construction is addressed by Simultaneous Localisation and Mapping (SLAM) algorithms.",
